# Editor / Dev-Only Systems

Types guarded by `#if UNITY_EDITOR || DEVELOPMENT_BUILD` **do not exist in a release player**. A release build must never load a prefab/scene that still references them — that yields shipped "missing script" / serialization-layout errors that never appear in the editor.

Two ways to satisfy the constraint — pick by whether the tool has UI.

## A. Prefab-authored, stripped at build time (preferred for UI-bearing tools)

Author the tool on a prefab and delete it from the scene during non-development builds with `IProcessSceneWithReport` (runs on the build's temp scene copy; committed scenes keep their instance).

Strip on a **marker component on the prefab root** — an empty `MonoBehaviour` under the same compile guard as the tools it marks:

```csharp
#if UNITY_EDITOR || DEVELOPMENT_BUILD
public sealed class DevToolsRoot : MonoBehaviour
{
}
#endif
```

```csharp
public sealed class DevToolsSceneStripper : IProcessSceneWithReport
{
    public int callbackOrder => 0;

    public void OnProcessScene(Scene scene, BuildReport report)
    {
        // Null report means play mode is starting, not a build — the tools must survive.
        if (report == null || report.summary.options.HasFlag(BuildOptions.Development))
        {
            return;
        }

        GameObject[] roots = scene.GetRootGameObjects();
        for (int i = 0; i < roots.Length; i++)
        {
            if (roots[i].GetComponentInChildren<DevToolsRoot>(true) != null)
            {
                Object.DestroyImmediate(roots[i]);
            }
        }
    }
}
```

- `report == null` early-out is load-bearing — Unity also runs scene processors on play entry.
- **Match on a component type, never on GameObject name.**
- **One marker over a list of every dev type** — forgetting to update a type list is invisible in the editor and ships as missing scripts.
- **Same `#if` guard on every component sharing the prefab**, marker included. A narrower `UNITY_EDITOR`-only sibling becomes a missing script in *development* players.
- Adding a tool to an existing marked prefab needs no stripper edit.

## B. Self-spawn under the guard (small, no-UI, always-on helpers)

When there is no authorable content, a prefab buys nothing. Guard the file and create itself:

```csharp
#if UNITY_EDITOR || DEVELOPMENT_BUILD
using UnityEngine;

public sealed class DevLogForwarder : MonoBehaviour
{
    // Self-spawns: type is compiled out of release, so a serialized ref would become a
    // missing script in the player. Idempotent — bails if one already exists.
    [RuntimeInitializeOnLoadMethod(RuntimeInitializeLoadType.AfterSceneLoad)]
    private static void EnsureInstance()
    {
        if (FindAnyObjectByType<DevLogForwarder>(FindObjectsInactive.Include) != null)
        {
            return;
        }

        new GameObject(nameof(DevLogForwarder)).AddComponent<DevLogForwarder>();
    }
}
#endif
```

- Bootstrap must be **idempotent** (`AfterSceneLoad` fires every scene load).
- Code defaults only; no inspector data on a self-spawned instance.
- Once a tool grows UI or becomes optional per scene, migrate to A — self-spawn forces it into every scene and can only build UI in code.

| Situation | Pattern |
|---|---|
| UI, optional per scene, or Inspector tuning | **A** — prefab + stripper |
| Few lines, no UI, always on in dev | **B** — self-spawn |
| Several tools in one project | **A** — one shared prefab, one stripper |

## Do not

- Ship a guarded `MonoBehaviour` on a prefab/scene without a covering stripper.
- Mix compile guards on one dev prefab — the narrowest guard decides where it breaks.
- Strip by name, or strip without checking `BuildOptions.Development`.
- Rely on `[SerializeField]` for a **self-spawned** component.
- Leave an orphan prefab that only hosted a now-self-spawning component.

## Agent checklist

- [ ] No `#if UNITY_EDITOR || DEVELOPMENT_BUILD` MonoBehaviour reachable from a shipped prefab/scene without a stripper
- [ ] Prefab-hosted tools: same guard on all components; root carries the stripper marker
- [ ] Stripper checks `report == null` and `BuildOptions.Development`; matches by type
- [ ] Self-spawned systems are guarded, idempotent, and work from code defaults
- [ ] Orphan prefabs for now-self-spawning components are removed
